WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.329 --> 00:00:19.091
was lying on the floor of Grandpa's study one rainy afternoon, doing her homework.

2
00:00:20.192 --> 00:00:24.074
She saw a crumpled piece of paper near the trash can and smoothed it out.

3
00:00:24.094 --> 00:00:26.815
"Hey, Grandpa, what's this?

4
00:00:27.875 --> 00:00:28.635
Did you draw it?"

5
00:00:29.096 --> 00:00:32.914
she asked, looking at a drawing of a vicious-looking man on a horse.

6
00:00:32.934 --> 00:00:37.317
"That is the work of a talented student, not me.

7
00:00:38.418 --> 00:00:44.141
He found it more entertaining to draw Attila the Hun than to listen to my lecture on Hun

8
00:00:44.261 --> 00:00:46.102
artifacts," Grandpa said.

9
00:00:46.122 --> 00:00:48.684
"Attila the Hun?

10
00:00:49.725 --> 00:00:50.605
I've heard of him!

11
00:00:51.626 --> 00:00:53.527
Wasn't he a horrible villain?"

12
00:00:53.847 --> 00:00:54.628
Laurie asked.

13
00:00:55.804 --> 00:01:00.768
"Well, it depends on who you ask," Grandpa said, joining Laurie on the floor.

14
00:01:00.788 --> 00:01:08.353
"The Huns were a group of nomadic horsemen who lived on the steppes of western central Asia.

15
00:01:09.494 --> 00:01:15.258
Most of what we know about them was written down by men of the Roman Empire—the empire the

16
00:01:15.298 --> 00:01:16.158
Huns invaded.

17
00:01:17.679 --> 00:01:23.503
Since the Huns had no written history of their own, it is difficult for historians to explain their

18
00:01:23.523 --> 00:01:29.874
past. But we know they lived in big tents made of wooden frames covered in wool.

19
00:01:30.914 --> 00:01:36.717
And they moved around when the seasons changed to find new grazing land for their large herds of

20
00:01:36.817 --> 00:01:40.498
cattle, sheep, and horses, which they depended on for food.

21
00:01:41.639 --> 00:01:45.220
And we know that they had a big impact on the Roman Empire."

22
00:01:46.401 --> 00:01:49.442
Laurie heard a rumble of thunder and looked outside.

23
00:01:50.462 --> 00:01:52.063
The sky had grown dark.

24
00:01:53.684 --> 00:01:57.177
"Meanwhile the Romans lived in busy cities.

25
00:01:58.358 --> 00:02:03.261
They were developing governments, conquering neighboring lands, and organizing taxes.

26
00:02:04.441 --> 00:02:07.523
Imagine what they must have thought when they encountered the Huns!

27
00:02:08.704 --> 00:02:11.285
They couldn't understand their language or way of life.

28
00:02:12.486 --> 00:02:19.630
So they called these very different people barbarians, which is actually what they called anyone who was not

29
00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:20.290
from Rome."

30
00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:26.141
"When I eat with my hands, Mom says, 'Stop acting like a barbarian!'"

31
00:02:26.521 --> 00:02:27.222
Laurie said.

32
00:02:27.242 --> 00:02:30.844
"She just doesn't understand my way of eating!"

33
00:02:34.866 --> 00:02:35.767
Grandpa laughed.

34
00:02:35.787 --> 00:02:38.548
"But don't tell your mother I said that.

35
00:02:39.629 --> 00:02:41.050
Anyway, back to the Huns.

36
00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:44.892
They were the greatest horsemen in the world at the time.

37
00:02:46.153 --> 00:02:51.516
Old tales say that Hun children were taught to ride horses soon after they learned to walk.

38
00:02:52.992 --> 00:02:56.855
By the time they were adults, they were fast, skilled riders.

39
00:02:57.995 --> 00:03:01.958
They had to be, because horses were their main means of transportation.

40
00:03:02.999 --> 00:03:05.781
Hun warriors also fought on horseback.

41
00:03:06.561 --> 00:03:12.645
They would race into villages, firing arrows, looking larger than life, and terrifying the villagers.

42
00:03:13.786 --> 00:03:20.070
It is said that Hun warriors cut and scarred their faces in order to make themselves look more

43
00:03:20.251 --> 00:03:28.398
frightening!" Laurie jumped as a bright flash of lightning lit the sky, followed by a clap of thunder.

44
00:03:28.418 --> 00:03:38.701
"Around the fourth century, the Huns began moving west—like a dark storm cloud—raining terror on everything in their

45
00:03:38.721 --> 00:03:44.703
path. Maybe they needed more land for their herds or maybe they were driven away by the strong

46
00:03:44.763 --> 00:03:48.144
walls China had built to keep them off Chinese territory.

47
00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:55.804
The important thing is that when the Huns moved west, they forced other barbarians off their land.

48
00:03:57.004 --> 00:04:01.467
So then these other barbarian tribes had to move farther west.

49
00:04:03.048 --> 00:04:06.870
They moved into the Roman Empire, upsetting cities there.

50
00:04:09.531 --> 00:04:12.193
Some tribes became part of the Roman Empire.

51
00:04:12.893 --> 00:04:14.514
Some lived right on the borders.

52
00:04:15.594 --> 00:04:17.976
And others just caused trouble.

53
00:04:19.104 --> 00:04:25.026
The Visigoths, one powerful tribe, were farmers who needed land to grow crops.

54
00:04:26.026 --> 00:04:30.527
After fighting Roman armies for some years, they settled within the empire.

55
00:04:32.067 --> 00:04:36.129
In exchange for land and peace, they defended the empire's borders.

56
00:04:37.229 --> 00:04:43.010
But the Visigoths soon grew unhappy because the Romans didn't give them everything they had promised.

57
00:04:44.131 --> 00:04:51.874
In the fourth century, the fearless Visigoth leader named Alaric led his soldiers into cities in what is

58
00:04:52.035 --> 00:04:53.476
now Italy and Greece.

59
00:04:54.637 --> 00:04:58.340
The Visigoths invaded, stole what they wanted, and left.

60
00:04:59.361 --> 00:05:01.022
This is called sacking.

61
00:05:03.344 --> 00:05:07.427
In 410 AD the Visigoths sacked Rome.

62
00:05:08.548 --> 00:05:13.012
It was a huge shock to the Romans, and many feared it would be the end of the

63
00:05:13.092 --> 00:05:14.713
greatest empire in the world."

64
00:05:14.733 --> 00:05:16.435
"Was it?"

65
00:05:16.915 --> 00:05:17.576
asked Laurie.

66
00:05:18.736 --> 00:05:25.139
"No. The Roman Empire held on for a while longer, and the barbarian invasions continued.

67
00:05:26.239 --> 00:05:29.080
The next major attack was from the Huns."

68
00:05:30.261 --> 00:05:33.622
Grandpa took something out of his pocket and tossed it to Laurie.

69
00:05:33.642 --> 00:05:35.083
"Heads up!"

70
00:05:36.383 --> 00:05:38.864
She opened her palm and saw it was a coin.

71
00:05:38.884 --> 00:05:46.308
"In the fifth century, Attila the Hun—the man on that coin you're holding—rose to power.

72
00:05:47.392 --> 00:05:52.213
He gathered thousands of Hun warriors and led them into southeastern Europe.

73
00:05:53.374 --> 00:06:00.896
After he conquered those lands, he moved his armies into Roman territories in modern-day Poland, Hungary, and Germany.

74
00:06:02.696 --> 00:06:08.218
The Huns became wealthy by looting cities, but also by taking prisoners and selling them.

75
00:06:08.898 --> 00:06:11.038
And they made money from peace treaties.

76
00:06:12.139 --> 00:06:17.060
These treaties often required the Romans to pay large amounts of money to the Huns.

77
00:06:18.336 --> 00:06:22.259
One treaty demanded nine hundred kilograms of gold a year!"

78
00:06:22.279 --> 00:06:24.521
"So what happened?

79
00:06:25.501 --> 00:06:29.344
Did the Huns become the richest, most powerful people in the world?"

80
00:06:29.724 --> 00:06:30.525
Laurie asked.

81
00:06:30.545 --> 00:06:35.529
"They didn't, although it seemed possible at one point.

82
00:06:36.349 --> 00:06:39.612
Attila the Hun died in 453 AD.

83
00:06:40.712 --> 00:06:45.436
He died of something silly—a nosebleed, they say—on his wedding night.

84
00:06:46.636 --> 00:06:50.339
How sad for a warrior who had survived so many battles.

85
00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:56.523
Shortly after that a civil war broke out among the Huns, destroying their empire.

86
00:06:57.624 --> 00:07:03.048
But the damage they and the other barbarians had done to the Roman Empire had weakened it forever.

87
00:07:04.649 --> 00:07:06.370
It, too, soon collapsed.

88
00:07:07.071 --> 00:07:10.493
The different regions created new governments and boundaries.

89
00:07:11.734 --> 00:07:14.696
Slowly Europe as we know it began to take shape."

90
00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:19.761
Lightning flashed, thunder boomed, and suddenly the lights went out.

91
00:07:21.302 --> 00:07:22.742
"Ahh!" Laurie shrieked.

92
00:07:23.822 --> 00:07:26.563
Grandpa reached for a candle by the window and lit it.

93
00:07:26.583 --> 00:07:30.204
"Here I am, Laurie."

94
00:07:31.224 --> 00:07:33.285
He put his arm around his granddaughter.

95
00:07:33.305 --> 00:07:41.767
"And if a scar-faced barbarian comes crashing through that door, don't worry—I'll protect you!"

96
00:07:42.947 --> 00:07:44.808
He held the candle up like a torch.

97
00:07:46.021 --> 00:07:48.264
"Grandpa!" Laurie giggled.

98
00:07:49.225 --> 00:07:50.767
"Laurie? Grandpa?

99
00:07:51.508 --> 00:07:52.950
Are you guys okay up there?"

100
00:07:53.531 --> 00:07:55.113
Dad shouted from downstairs.

101
00:07:55.133 --> 00:07:57.296
"We're okay, Dad!

102
00:07:57.316 --> 00:08:01.402
Safe and sound," Laurie answered.
